<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="PowerPC:BE:32:e500">
<!--
    GH-6904: "PowerQUICC-III-e500 Register Size Issues"
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/6904

    The PowerPC:BE:32:e500 language models the 32 general-purpose registers as
    64-bit (rX, REGISTER_SIZE 8 / BIT_64) because the SPE (Signal Processing
    Engine) instructions genuinely use the full 64-bit register pairs.  But the
    32-bit Book E integer instructions were lifted as if they operated on the
    full 64-bit rX, so the upper word is never cleared.  A 32-bit parameter that
    flows through `mr rD,rS` (encoded as `or` / `or.`) at a function boundary
    carries an UNINITIALISED upper 32 bits, and the decompiler reconstructs the
    value as CONCAT44(upper, lower) and an int8/ulonglong type.

    Repro (issue's e500_test/e500_bug_1_O2.so, function some_funcptr_func):
      some_funcptr_func(void **ptr, int call):
        mr. r31,r3        (= or. r31,r3,r3)   ; ptr (r3) copied 64-bit-wide
        if (CONCAT44(in_r3,ptr) != 0) ...     ; the bug

    Fix (kuna GH-6904): for the E500 variant, `or` / `or.` (the `mr`/`mr.`
    register-move idiom, the dominant boundary-widening op) zero-extend a 32-bit
    result so the upper word of rD is cleared.  Guarded by @ifdef E500, so the
    32-bit-register PowerPC:BE:32:default model is untouched.

    NOTE (scope): this is a faithful but PARTIAL fix for a structural problem.
    It removes the CONCAT44 in some_funcptr_func (the issue's repro target and
    bug_regex), but a complete fix would have to clear the upper word in every
    32-bit Book E integer constructor (add/cmpwi/bdnz/loads, ~239 sites): a
    whole-instruction-set re-lift outside the SLEIGH-scope rule.

    Stage: P-code lift (SLEIGH spec-fix, no option).  Asserts the FIXED output:
    no CONCAT44 over r3, ptr tested as a plain 32-bit pointer.

    bl at 0x620 patched 48000001 to 480000e1 so the relocated call to
    get_funcptr (0x700) resolves inside this self-contained chunk, matching the
    relocated .so the issue's headless repro analyses.
-->
  <bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x5fc" readonly="true">
9421ffe093e1001c7c7f1b794182005093c100187c0802a67c9e23783860000090010024480000e12c1e0000907f000040820020800100247c6903a683c1001883e1001c382100207c0803a64e8004208001002483c100187c0803a683e1001c382100204e800020
  </bytechunk>
  <bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x700" readonly="true">
